---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const propCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Real vs personal property', note: 'Real = land and everything fixed to it; personal = everything else (chattels, cars, ideas-ish). Different rules, different courts, different history — feudalism\'s ghost survives in land law.' },
    { math: '2 · Estates: slices through time', note: 'Land ownership is really holding time-periods: fee simple (forever), lease (years), life estate (until death). "Owning" land is owning a bundle of rights — sell, lease, mortgage, bequeath — separately.' },
    { math: '3 · Interests and easements', note: 'Others can hold rights OVER your land: easements (right of way), covenants (promises that bind successors), mortgages (lender\'s security). The bundle-of-rights is genuinely bundle-shaped.' },
    { math: '4 · Intellectual property: owning the intangible', note: 'Patents (inventions, ~20 years), copyright (expression, life+70), trademarks (brands, renewable forever), trade secrets (as long as kept secret). Different durations by design — ideas want to spread, so the monopoly is limited on purpose.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/advanced/property-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Property law defines <strong>what ownership means — and shows it was never one thing</strong>.
    Owning land means holding a bundle of rights (use, exclude, transfer, bequeath) sliced
    through time and shared with neighbours, lenders and the state. The same logic extends to the
    intangible: patents, copyright and trademarks are property engineered to balance creators
    against the public.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bundle of rights, from land to intellectual property" widget="equationStep" config={propCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts with long shadows</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adverse possession ("squatters' rights"):</strong> possess land openly for long enough and you can acquire title — law rewards the diligent owner over the sleeping one.</li>
    <li><strong>Tenure and registration:</strong> most systems register ownership in state records (Torrens system) — certainty by database rather than by chain of documents.</li>
    <li><strong>IP's balancing act:</strong> patents demand public disclosure in exchange for temporary monopoly — the bargain that built industrial innovation. Copyright's term extensions keep raising the "public domain" question.</li>
    <li><strong>Takings:</strong> the state can compulsory-purchase land — with compensation. Where that line sits (and what counts as a "taking" by regulation) is permanent constitutional argument.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gray & Gray, Elements of Land Law; standard IP texts (Bently & Sherman); Honoré, "Ownership" (1961) on the bundle.</p>
</TopicPage>
